84,"d":1946},{"n":"3 Baero Mart","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":35.091839,"lo":129.066264,"d":1982},{"n":"Busan National Livestock Cooperative Federation","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":35.091292,"lo":129.063075,"d":2134},{"n":"Cheonghakbik Mart","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":35.091085,"lo":129.061878,"d":2199},{"n":"영도새마을금고","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":35.094706,"lo":129.064575,"d":2328},{"n":"Busan Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.100556,"lo":129.072778,"d":2592,"ds":"Busan Bay (Korean: 부산만) is a bay located in the southern Busan, South Korea. The entrance of the bay opens to the southeast, and leads to the Korea Strait. The bay has a number of islands in it, including Oryukdo, Yeongdo, Dudo, and Jodo.","wpu":"Busan Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Busan-IMGP7037.jpg/960px-Busan-IMGP7037.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Port of Busan","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.103889,"lo":129.078889,"d":2880,"ds":"The port of Busan is the largest and busiest port in South Korea, located in the city of Busan. Its location is known as Busan Harbour. Busan handled over three-quarters of Korea's container shipping in 2022, and by 2023 it was the sixth-busiest port in the world.","wpu":"Port of Busan","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Busan-port-from-Busan-tower-2.jpg/960px-Busan-port-from-Busan-tower-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Taejongdae","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.05,"lo":129.083056,"d":3125,"ds":"Taejongdae (Korean: 태종대) is a natural park on the southernmost point of Yeongdo District, Busan, South Korea. It is a popular tourist attraction in Busan, with dense evergreen trees and facilities for tourists such as an observatory, an amusement park, a light house, and a cruise ship terminal.","wpu":"Taejongdae","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Korea-Busan-Taejongdae-03.jpg/960px-Korea-Busan-Taejongdae-03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Namhang Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.080278,"lo":129.031944,"d":4380,"ds":"Namhangdaegyo (남항대교) is a girder bridge in Busan. It spans Yeongdo-gu and Seo-gu and is 1.8 km long in total with four lanes of traffic (two in each direction).","wpu":"Namhang Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Busan_-_Namhang_bridge.jpg/960px-Busan_-_Namhang_bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yeongdo Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.095556,"lo":129.036667,"d":4399,"ds":"Yeongdodaegyo A.K.A. Yeongdodari is a bascule bridge in Yeongdo, Busan, Republic of Korea. It is also called Yeongdo Bridge in English. Completed in 1934, it connects Jung District ward on Busan mainland to Yeongdo District on the Yeongdo Island spanning the south port of Busan.","wpu":"Yeongdo Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Yeongdodaegyo%28Yeongdo_Bridge%29.jpg/960px-Yeongdodaegyo%28Yeongdo_Bridge%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Busan Lotte Town Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.097517,"lo":129.037317,"d":4448,"ds":"The Busan Lotte Tower (Korean: 부산롯데타워) is a 108-floor, 510.1 m (1,674 ft) supertall skyscraper on hold in Busan, South Korea. The tower is planned on a site next to Nampo-dong station on Busan Subway Line 1. The tower is the centerpiece of the new Busan Lotte Town and construction is being carried out in four phases.","wpu":"Busan Lotte Town Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d8/Construction_site_of_Busan_Lotte_Tower_1.jpg/960px-Construction_site_of_Busan_Lotte_Tower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Oryukdo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.093056,"lo":129.126667,"d":4564,"ds":"Oryukdo (Korean: 오륙도; lit. 'Five–Six Island[s]') is a group of islands in Busan Bay, in Yongho-dong, Nam District, Busan, South Korea. The name of the islands reflects the fact that they can consist of either five or six islands, depending on the current tides.","wpu":"Oryukdo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Oryukdo_in_2018.jpg/960px-Oryukdo_in_2018.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Jagalchi Market","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.096667,"lo":129.03,"d":5000,"ds":"Jagalchi Fish Market (Korean: 자갈치시장) is a fish market in Busan, South Korea. The market is located on the edge of Nampo Port (남포항), Busan. It is considered to be the largest fish market in South Korea.","wpu":"Jagalchi Market","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Jagalchi_Market_20200523_019.jpg/960px-Jagalchi_Market_20200523_019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Busan Cooperative Fish Market","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.0884,"lo":129.0252,"d":5118,"ds":"The Busan Cooperative Fish Market (BCFM; Korean: 부산공동어시장) is the largest fish market in South Korea. It adjoins the South Harbor in the city of Busan, and more than 30% of the country's fish production passes through the market. In recent years, a large percentage of the catch has been made up of yellowtail, due to warming waters in the Sea of Japan.","wpu":"Busan Cooperative Fish Market","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Busan_Hafen_Fischmarkt_2.jpg/960px-Busan_Hafen_Fischmarkt_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"National Memorial Museum of Forced Mobilization under Japanese Occupation","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.124972,"lo":129.092333,"d":5342,"ds":"The National Memorial Museum of Forced Mobilization under Japanese Occupation (Korean: 국립일제강제동원역사관) is a national history museum in Busan, South Korea. It first opened on December 10, 2015. It covers the Japanese colonial period (1910–1945), in particular when Koreans were forced to perform labor and moved to other places to support Japan.","wpu":"National Memorial Museum of Forced Mobilization under Japanese Occupation","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/eb/National_Memorial_Museum_of_Forced_Mobilization_under_Japanese_Occupation.JPG/960px-National_Memorial_Museum_of_Forced_Mobilization_under_Japanese_Occupation.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Gukje Market","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.101,"lo":129.028,"d":5378,"ds":"Gukje Market (Korean: 국제시장) or Nampodong International Market is a market in Sinchang-dong, Jung District, Busan, South Korea. It was originally known by the name Dottaegi Market, and later as Jayu Market. The market was founded in 1945 under the name Dotegi Market in an empty lot.","wpu":"Gukje Market","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Gukje_Market%2C_October_2019_03.jpg/960px-Gukje_Market%2C_October_2019_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Songdo Beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.07619,"lo":129.01803,"d":5642,"ds":"Songdo Beach (Korean: 송도해수욕장) is a man-made beach in Busan, South Korea. It was created in 1913. The name \"Songdo\" (the Island of Pines) was given due to the thick pine forest covering the island.","wpu":"Songdo Beach","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Busan_-_Songdo_beach.jpg/960px-Busan_-_Songdo_beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"United Nations Memorial Cemetery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.128056,"lo":129.096944,"d":5775,"ds":"The United Nations Memorial Cemetery in Korea (UNMCK; Korean: 재한유엔기념공원; RR: Jaehan Yuen ginyeomgongwon), located at Tanggok in the Nam District, of Busan, South Korea, is a burial ground for United Nations Command (UNC) casualties of the Korean War. It contains 2,300 graves and is the only United Nations cemetery in the world.","wpu":"United Nations Memorial Cemetery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Sites_of_the_Busan_Wartime_Capital_United_Nations_Memorial_Cemetery_%28United_Nations_Memorial_Cemetry_in_Korea%29.jpg/960px-Sites_of_the_Busan_Wartime_Capital_United_Nations_Memorial_Cemetery_%28United_Nations_Memorial_Cemetry_in_Korea%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Provisional Capital Memorial Hall","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.103345,"lo":129.017306,"d":6362,"ds":"The Provisional Capital Memorial Hall (Korean: 임시수도기념관; Hanja: 臨時首都記念館) is a museum in Bumin-dong, Seo District, Busan, South Korea. The building was used by the President of South Korea, Syngman Rhee, when Busan was the provisional capital of South Korea during the Korean War.","wpu":"Provisional Capital Memorial Hall","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Provisional_Capital_Memorial_Hall_11-10469.jpg/960px-Provisional_Capital_Memorial_Hall_11-10469.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Busanjinseong","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.136111,"lo":129.061111,"d":6686,"ds":"The Branch Wall-fortress in Busanjin (釜山鎭城), also known as Maruyama Castle (丸山城, まるやまじょう) and Konishi Castle (少西城), is located at Beomil-dong, Dong-gu, Busan, South Korea. The existing wall-fortress remains were constructed by the Japanese military during the Imjin War. There are two assertions on the name of Jaseong (Subordinate Castle).","wpu":"Busanjinseong","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/%EB%B6%80%EC%82%B0%EC%A7%84%EC%84%B1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Busanjin Market","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.1364,"lo":129.0587,"d":6776,"ds":"Busanjin Market (Korean: 부산진시장; Hanja: 釜山鎭市場) is a traditional market in Beomil-dong, Dong District, Busan, South Korea.","wpu":"Busanjin Market","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Busanjin_sijang_in_2018.jpg/960px-Busanjin_sijang_in_2018.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Busan , officially Busan Metropolitan City, is the second most populous city in South Korea, after Seoul; it has a population of over 3.3 million as of 2024. It is the economic, cultural and educational center of southeastern South Korea, with its port being South Korea's busiest and the sixth-busiest in the world. The surrounding \"Southeastern Maritime Industrial Region\" (including Ulsan, South Gyeongsang, Daegu, and part of North Gyeongsang and South Jeolla) is South Korea's largest industrial area.","wpu":"Busan","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Busan_City_Haeundae_District_%2801%29.jpg/960px-Busan_City_Haeundae_District_%2801%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}